ls) in pair 2. The fact sheet confirms the output is an exact crop of the input at exactly that shape's bounding box — (9,11)–(11,13) for the 4-shape, (10,17)–(14,19) for the 3-shape. The noise dots never enter the crop (the bounding boxes contain only the shape colour and 0), so the output is simply the big shape cut out of the grid, with 0 remaining in the holes of its box.\n\n**How to identify the shape generally.** The noise objects are all size ≤ 2, while the special shape is clearly the largest connected component; it is also the only object of its colour. The simplest robust choice is \"the largest non-background object\", which picks the 4-shape, the 3-shape, and in the test the colour-2 shape (12 cells, bbox [12,19]–[15,22]) while all other test objects are size ≤ 4.\n\n**Construction stages.** (1) Allocate a canvas the size of the shape's bounding box, filled with background. (2) Stamp the shape's cells into it at their offsets from the box corner. This yields the 3×3 and 5×3 outputs for the train pairs; for the test it yields a 4×4 grid showing the colour-2 ring-like shape.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    grid = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(grid)\n    objs = objects(grid, bg=bg)\n    # the special shape is the largest connected object; everything else is tiny noise\n    target = max(objs, key=lambda o: o[\"size\"])\n    y0, x0, y1, x1 = target[\"box\"]\n    h, w = y1 - y0 + 1, x1 - x0 + 1\n\n    # stage 1: allocate an empty canvas the size of the shape's bounding box\n    canvas = np.full((h, w), bg, dtype=int)\n    emit(canvas, f\"Allocate a {h}x{w} canvas (the bounding box of the largest object, colour {target['colour']}) filled with background {bg}\")\n\n    # stage 2: stamp the shape's cells into the canvas\n    for (y, x) in target[\"cells\"]:\n        canvas[y - y0, x - x0] = target[\"colour\"]\n    emit(canvas, f\"Stamp the {target['size']} cells of the colour-{target['colour']} shape into the canvas; holes stay background\")\n\n    return canvas\n","candidate_tactics":{"objects":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"ape generally.** The noise objects are all size ≤ 2, while the special shape is clearly the largest connected component; it is also the only object of its colour."}],"selection":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"y the shape generally.** The noise objects are all size ≤ 2, while the special shape is clearly the largest connected component; it is also the only object of its colour."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"The simplest robust choice is \"the largest non-background object\", which picks the 4-shape, the 3-shape, and in the test the colour-2 shape (12 cells, bbox [12,19]–[15,22]) while all other test objects are size ≤ 4."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Allocate a 3x3 canvas (the bounding box of the largest object, colour 4) filled with background 0"}],"template":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"n stages.** (1) Allocate a canvas the size of the shape's bounding box, filled with background. (2) Stamp the shape's cells into it at their offsets from the box corner."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Stamp the 6 cells of the colour-4 shape into the canvas; holes stay background"},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Stamp the 12 cells of the colour-3 shape into the canvas; holes stay background"}],"topology":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"e colour and 0), so the output is simply the big shape cut out of the grid, with 0 remaining in the holes of its box."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Stamp the 6 cells of the colour-4 shape into the canvas; holes stay background"},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Stamp the 12 cells of the colour-3 shape into the canvas; holes stay background"}],"crop":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"The fact sheet confirms the output is an exact crop of the input at exactly that shape's bounding box — (9,11)–(11,13) for the 4-shape, (10,17)–(14,19) for the 3-s